<!-- Section 1: sec-intro -->
<section id="sec-intro" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Right Triangle Circumradius Calculator</h2>
  <p>Find the circumradius of a right triangle in one division: halve the hypotenuse.</p>
  <p>The circumradius is the radius of the circle that passes through all three vertices of the triangle, called the circumcircle. The hypotenuse c is the side opposite the right angle, and it is the only measurement this calculator needs, because the hypotenuse is also the diameter of the circumcircle. The formula is <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{c}{2}]]')} />, where <span set:html={rich('[[R]]')} /> is the circumradius and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the complete hypotenuse.</p>
  <p>For example, if <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> cm, then <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{10}{2} = 5]]')} /> cm. That 5 cm is the distance from the center of the circumcircle to each vertex of the triangle. The same distance reaches vertex A, vertex B, and vertex C, which is exactly what makes the circle pass through all three.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 2: sec-calculate -->
<section id="sec-calculate" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Calculate the Circumradius of a Right Triangle</h2>
  <p>The calculator on this page has one input field, for the hypotenuse c. The hypotenuse is the side opposite the right angle, and it is also the longest side of the triangle. Measure it from one end to the other, in whatever unit you are using, and enter that single number.</p>
  <p>The hypotenuse alone is enough because the circumradius depends on nothing else. That is why the calculator needs no second or third input.</p>
  <p>Enter a positive length. A hypotenuse of zero or a negative number describes no real triangle, and the calculator has nothing sensible to halve. Keep the unit consistent with your problem: if c is in centimeters, the answer is in centimeters, and if c is in meters, the answer is in meters.</p>
  <p>Click Calculate Circumradius R to see the result, and use Reset to clear the field before a new problem. Read the answer as a radius, not a diameter. If the calculator shows 5 cm for a hypotenuse of 10 cm, the full circle across is 10 cm, and 5 cm is the distance from its center to its edge.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 3: sec-what-circumradius -->
<section id="sec-what-circumradius" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is the Circumradius of a Right Triangle?</h2>
  <p>The circumradius of a right triangle is the radius of its circumcircle, the circle drawn through all three vertices. Take triangle ABC with the right angle at C. Draw a circle that passes exactly through A, B, and C. The distance from the center of that circle to any one of the three vertices is the circumradius, written <span set:html={rich('[[R]]')} />.</p>
  <p>The center of the circle is called the circumcenter, written <span set:html={rich('[[O]]')} />. In a right triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[O]]')} /> sits at the midpoint of the hypotenuse AB. Because <span set:html={rich('[[O]]')} /> is the center, the distances from <span set:html={rich('[[O]]')} /> to the three vertices are all equal, so <span set:html={rich('[[AO = BO = CO = R]]')} />. One number, <span set:html={rich('[[R]]')} />, describes all three distances at once.</p>
  <p>A simple example makes it concrete. In a 6-8-10 triangle, the hypotenuse is 10 cm, so the circumradius is 5 cm. The center sits 5 cm from A, 5 cm from B, and 5 cm from C. The legs, 6 cm and 8 cm, are sides of the triangle, not radii, and neither one equals the circumradius here.</p>
