    <img src={imgSrc('circumradius-main-circumcircle.svg')} alt="Right triangle ABC inside its circumcircle with center O at the midpoint of hypotenuse AB" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Triangle ABC inside its circumcircle: O is the center, and AO, BO, and CO each equal R.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 4: sec-circumcircle -->
<section id="sec-circumcircle" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is a Circumcircle in Right Triangle Geometry?</h2>
  <p>A circumcircle is a circle that passes through every vertex of a polygon. For a triangle, that means the circle goes through all three corners and nowhere else is required of it. Every triangle has exactly one circumcircle, because three noncollinear points determine a single circle.</p>
  <p>In a right triangle, the circumcircle has a special shape. The triangle fits inside the circle with its hypotenuse stretched straight across the middle, from one side of the circle to the other. The third vertex, the right-angle vertex C, sits on the circle between them. No part of the triangle sticks out beyond the circle, and the circle is the smallest one that can pass through all three vertices.</p>
  <p>The circumcircle is different from the incircle. The incircle sits inside the triangle and touches all three sides from within. The circumcircle surrounds the triangle and touches it only at the three vertices. Mixing the two circles up is the most common source of confusion on this page, and the comparison section below sets them side by side.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 5: sec-formula -->
<section id="sec-formula" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Right Triangle Circumradius Formula</h2>
  <p>The formula for the circumradius of a right triangle is short enough to remember at a glance:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[R = \\frac{c}{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Here <span set:html={rich('[[R]]')} /> is the circumradius, the radius of the circumcircle, and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the complete hypotenuse, the side opposite the right angle. The formula says: take the hypotenuse and divide it by two.</p>
  <p>The calculation has two steps. First, identify the hypotenuse correctly. It is the side across from the right angle, and it is always the longest side. Second, divide its length by two. There is no squaring, no square root, and no second measurement to find first, provided the hypotenuse is already known.</p>
  <p>The reason the division works is that the hypotenuse is the diameter of the circumcircle. A radius is always half a diameter, so <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{D}{2}]]')} />, where <span set:html={rich('[[D]]')} /> is the diameter. In a right triangle <span set:html={rich('[[D = c]]')} />, so substituting gives <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{c}{2}]]')} />. The formula is really the familiar radius-diameter relationship, applied to a diameter that happens to be a side of the triangle.</p>
  <p>For a general triangle, one that is not necessarily right-angled, the circumradius follows the formula <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{abc}{4A}]]')} />, where <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> are the three sides and <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> is the area of the triangle. For a right triangle it simplifies to <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{c}{2}]]')} />, because the area is <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\frac{ab}{2}]]')} />. Use the general formula only for triangles that are not right-angled.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 6: sec-why-diameter -->
<section id="sec-why-diameter" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Why Is the Hypotenuse the Diameter of the Circumcircle?</h2>
  <p>This is the idea the whole page turns on, so it deserves a careful look. Take right triangle ABC with the right angle at C, and let <span set:html={rich('[[O]]')} /> be the midpoint of the hypotenuse AB. The claim is that <span set:html={rich('[[O]]')} /> is equally distant from all three vertices, which makes it the center of the circumcircle, and that AB, passing through <span set:html={rich('[[O]]')} /> with both ends on the circle, is a diameter.</p>
  <p>The key fact is the midpoint property of a right triangle: the midpoint of the hypotenuse is equally distant from all three vertices. In symbols, <span set:html={rich('[[OA = OB = OC]]')} />. Since <span set:html={rich('[[O]]')} /> is the midpoint of AB, <span set:html={rich('[[OA]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[OB]]')} /> are each half the hypotenuse, so <span set:html={rich('[[OA = OB = \\frac{c}{2}]]')} />.</p>
  <p>Why does <span set:html={rich('[[OC]]')} /> equal half the hypotenuse? One way to see it is to complete the rectangle. Draw a copy of triangle ABC rotated half a turn about <span set:html={rich('[[O]]')} />, so that the two triangles together form a rectangle with AB as one diagonal. The diagonals of a rectangle are equal and bisect each other, so <span set:html={rich('[[OC = \\frac{c}{2}]]')} /> too.</p>
  <p>Once <span set:html={rich('[[OA = OB = OC]]')} /> is established, the rest follows. A circle centered at <span set:html={rich('[[O]]')} /> with radius <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{c}{2}]]')} /> passes through A, B, and C, so it is the circumcircle, <span set:html={rich('[[O]]')} /> is the circumcenter, and <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{c}{2}]]')} /> is the circumradius. Since A and B are opposite ends of a line through the center, AB is a diameter, so <span set:html={rich('[[AB = 2R]]')} />, which is <span set:html={rich('[[c = 2R]]')} /> and therefore <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{c}{2}]]')} />.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('circumradius-hypotenuse-diameter.svg')} alt="Hypotenuse AB shown as the diameter of the circumcircle with AO and OB equal to R" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Hypotenuse AB is the diameter: AO and OB each equal R, and AB equals 2R.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 7: sec-circumcenter -->
<section id="sec-circumcenter" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is the Circumcenter of a Right Triangle?</h2>
  <p>The circumcenter of a triangle is the point equally distant from all three vertices, and it is the center of the circumcircle. It is found where the perpendicular bisectors of the sides meet.</p>
  <p>For a right triangle, the perpendicular bisectors meet at a point you can find without drawing them: the midpoint of the hypotenuse. Bisect AB and you have the circumcenter <span set:html={rich('[[O]]')} />. Bisecting either leg gives a line that passes through the same point, which is a useful check when you are constructing the center with a compass and straightedge.</p>
  <p>The circumcenter lies on the hypotenuse itself, exactly halfway along it. That is the special feature of right triangles. In an acute triangle, all three angles are less than 90 degrees and the circumcenter falls inside the triangle. The right triangle is the boundary case, with the center sitting on the boundary of the triangle, on its longest side.</p>
  <p>The circumcenter is not the right-angle vertex. Vertex C is a point on the circle, at distance <span set:html={rich('[[R]]')} /> from <span set:html={rich('[[O]]')} />, while <span set:html={rich('[[O]]')} /> is the center. Confusing the two is a common error, because C is the most visually striking corner of the triangle. Remember that the center must be equally distant from A, B, and C, and C is not equally distant from A and B unless the triangle is isosceles in a particular way, so C cannot be the center in general.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('circumradius-circumcenter.svg')} alt="Circumcenter O at the midpoint of the hypotenuse, equally distant from all three vertices" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">O is the midpoint of AB, with OA, OB, and OC all equal to R.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 8: sec-how-to-use -->
<section id="sec-how-to-use" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Use the Right Triangle Circumradius Calculator</h2>
  <p>Nine short steps take you from a labeled triangle to a checked answer. The legs are not needed at any point, because the hypotenuse alone determines the circumradius.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Identify the right triangle in your problem and confirm it has a right angle.</li>
    <li>Locate the right angle, usually marked with a small square at vertex C.</li>
    <li>Identify the side opposite the right angle; that side is the hypotenuse c.</li>
    <li>Confirm that this side is the longest side of the triangle. If it is not, relabel the diagram before continuing.</li>
    <li>Select the appropriate unit and convert any measurements so they all use that unit.</li>
    <li>Enter the hypotenuse length in the Hypotenuse c field.</li>
    <li>Click Calculate Circumradius R and read the result.</li>
    <li>Check the result: it must be positive, in the same unit as c, and exactly half of c.</li>
    <li>Use Reset when starting another calculation.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>If your problem gives the two legs instead of the hypotenuse, find c first with the Pythagorean theorem, <span set:html={rich('[[c = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}]]')} />, and then enter that value. The <a href="/right-triangle-side-calculator/">right triangle side calculator</a> can find the missing hypotenuse for you, and the section on legs below works through a full example.</p>
</section>

