<section id="sec-step-example" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Step-by-Step Example of Finding Circumradius</h2>
  <p>Use <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> cm. The three steps below show the full working, with nothing skipped.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Write the formula: <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{c}{2}]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Substitute the known value: <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{10}{2}]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Divide: <span set:html={rich('[[R = 5]]')} /> cm.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The answer 5 cm means the circumcircle has a radius of 5 cm. Its center, the midpoint of the hypotenuse, sits 5 cm from each vertex of the triangle, and the circle passes through all three vertices. The diameter of the circle is 10 cm, the same as the hypotenuse.</p>
  <p>Verify the answer with the diameter relationship, <span set:html={rich('[[2R = c]]')} />. Doubling the radius gives <span set:html={rich('[[2 \\times 5 = 10]]')} /> cm, which matches the hypotenuse exactly. If doubling your answer does not return the hypotenuse you started with, the division went wrong somewhere, and the check catches it at once.</p>
