<!-- Section 10: sec-diagram -->
<section id="sec-diagram" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Right Triangle Circumcircle Diagram Explained</h2>
  <p>The diagram shows triangle ABC with the right angle at C. Side AC is leg a, side BC is leg b, and side AB is the hypotenuse c. The point <span set:html={rich('[[O]]')} /> marks the midpoint of AB, and a circle centered at <span set:html={rich('[[O]]')} /> passes through A, B, and C.</p>
  <p>Read the diagram in this order. Start at A, run along leg a to C, then along leg b to B, then back along the hypotenuse from B to A, passing through <span set:html={rich('[[O]]')} /> on the way. The segments from <span set:html={rich('[[O]]')} /> to A, B, and C are all radii, each labeled <span set:html={rich('[[R]]')} />.</p>
  <p>The segment AB passes through the center <span set:html={rich('[[O]]')} /> and has both endpoints on the circle, which is the definition of a diameter. That is why AB is drawn as a straight line across the middle of the circle rather than as a chord off to one side. The right angle at C sits on the circle itself, which is the picture Thales' theorem describes.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 11: sec-proof -->
<section id="sec-proof" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Why Does the Circumradius Formula Work?</h2>
  <p>The proof uses the midpoint of the hypotenuse and the fact that it is equally distant from all three vertices. Let <span set:html={rich('[[M]]')} /> be the midpoint of AB in right triangle ABC, with the right angle at C. Then <span set:html={rich('[[AM = BM = \\frac{c}{2}]]')} /> by the definition of a midpoint. The step that needs proof is <span set:html={rich('[[MA = MB = MC]]')} />, the claim that the third distance matches the first two.</p>
  <p>One clean way to show it is to extend the median CM to a point D so that <span set:html={rich('[[MD = MC]]')} />, and join D to A and B. The quadrilateral ACBD has diagonals AB and CD that bisect each other at <span set:html={rich('[[M]]')} />, so it is a parallelogram. Its angle at C is a right angle, and a parallelogram with one right angle is a rectangle, so ACBD is a rectangle.</p>
  <p>The diagonals of a rectangle are equal, so <span set:html={rich('[[CD = AB = c]]')} />. Since <span set:html={rich('[[M]]')} /> bisects CD, <span set:html={rich('[[MC = \\frac{CD}{2} = \\frac{c}{2}]]')} />. That matches <span set:html={rich('[[MA]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[MB]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[MA = MB = MC = \\frac{c}{2}]]')} />. The point <span set:html={rich('[[M]]')} /> is equally distant from A, B, and C, which makes it the circumcenter, and the common distance <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{c}{2}]]')} /> is the circumradius. Therefore <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{c}{2}]]')} />.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 12: sec-thales -->
<section id="sec-thales" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding Thales' Theorem and the Circumcircle</h2>
  <p>Thales' theorem states that an angle subtended by a diameter at a point on a circle is a right angle. In the picture, AB is a diameter of a circle and C is any point on the circle other than A or B. Then angle ACB is 90 degrees. The theorem is named after Thales of Miletus, who is credited with the first known proof of it.</p>
  <p>The converse is what this page uses: if a triangle has a right angle, its hypotenuse can be treated as the diameter of a circle passing through all three vertices. Take triangle ABC with angle C equal to 90 degrees. The converse says C lies on the circle with diameter AB, so that circle is the circumcircle of the triangle, and its radius is half of AB.</p>
  <p>The two directions together give a complete test. A point C on the circle with diameter AB always gives a right angle at C, and a right angle at C always puts C on that circle.</p>
  <p>The theorem also explains the circumcenter's position. The center of the circle is the midpoint of its diameter, and the diameter is the hypotenuse, so the center is the midpoint of the hypotenuse. Nothing else about the triangle needs to be known. That is why the calculator asks for only one number.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('circumradius-thales-theorem.svg')} alt="Thales theorem diagram with diameter AB and point C on the circle forming a right angle" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Thales' theorem: AB is the diameter, C is on the circle, and angle C is 90 degrees.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 13: sec-examples -->
<section id="sec-examples" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Worked Examples of Calculating Right Triangle Circumradius</h2>
  <h3>Example 1: Basic Whole Number</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> cm, the formula gives <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{10}{2} = 5]]')} /> cm. The circumcircle has a diameter of 10 cm and a radius of 5 cm, and its center is the midpoint of the hypotenuse, 5 cm from each vertex.</p>
  <h3>Example 2: Larger Measurement</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c = 24]]')} /> m, <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{24}{2} = 12]]')} /> m. Larger numbers change nothing about the method: halve the hypotenuse and keep the unit. A circle 24 m across has a radius of 12 m.</p>
  <h3>Example 3: Decimal Measurement</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c = 7.5]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{7.5}{2} = 3.75]]')} /> cm. A decimal hypotenuse gives a decimal circumradius, which is normal. Check it by doubling: <span set:html={rich('[[2 \\times 3.75 = 7.5]]')} /> cm, which returns the hypotenuse.</p>
  <h3>Example 4: Odd Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c = 13]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{13}{2} = 6.5]]')} /> cm. An odd whole number halves to a number ending in .5. This is the 5-12-13 triangle, whose legs are 5 cm and 12 cm, since <span set:html={rich('[[5^2 + 12^2 = 25 + 144 = 169 = 13^2]]')} />.</p>
  <h3>Example 5: Millimeter Measurement</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c = 180]]')} /> mm, <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{180}{2} = 90]]')} /> mm. Small units work the same way, and keeping the unit in millimeters throughout avoids any conversion. In centimeters, the same answer is 9 cm, since 90 mm equals 9 cm.</p>
  <h3>Example 6: Fractional Measurement</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c = \\frac{15}{2}]]')} /> ft, <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{15/2}{2} = \\frac{15}{4}]]')} /> ft, which is 3.75 ft. Dividing by 2 means multiplying the denominator by 2, so the denominator grows from 2 to 4. The decimal form, 3.75 ft, is the same answer written differently.</p>
  <h3>Example 7: Different Units</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c = 1.2]]')} /> m, <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{1.2}{2} = 0.6]]')} /> m. Converting to centimeters, 0.6 m equals 60 cm, because 1 m is 100 cm. Either unit is correct, but the answer must state which one it uses.</p>
  <h3>Example 8: Large Decimal Measurement</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c = 125.6]]')} /> m, <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{125.6}{2} = 62.8]]')} /> m. Doubling 62.8 returns 125.6, which confirms the division.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 14: sec-legs-known -->
<section id="sec-legs-known" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Find Circumradius When the Two Legs Are Known</h2>
  <p>The calculator on this page asks for the hypotenuse, but many problems give the two legs instead. In that case, find the hypotenuse first with the Pythagorean theorem, then halve it. The two steps combine into one formula, <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{\\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}}{2}]]')} />, where <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> are the legs.</p>
  <p>Use <span set:html={rich('[[a = 6]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[b = 8]]')} /> cm. First square both legs and add: <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 + b^2 = 36 + 64 = 100]]')} />. Then take the square root to get the hypotenuse: <span set:html={rich('[[c = \\sqrt{100} = 10]]')} /> cm. Then halve it: <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{10}{2} = 5]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p>The <a href="/pythagorean-theorem-calculator/">Pythagorean theorem calculator</a> finds a missing hypotenuse from two legs, which is the same first step used here. The <a href="/right-triangle-area-calculator/">right triangle area calculator</a> uses the same two legs in a different way, computing <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\frac{ab}{2}]]')} />, which is 24 square centimeters for the 6-8-10 triangle.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 15: sec-diameter -->
<section id="sec-diameter" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Relationship Between Circumradius and Diameter</h2>
  <p>Radius and diameter describe the same circle in two ways. The diameter <span set:html={rich('[[D]]')} /> is the distance straight across the circle through its center, and the radius <span set:html={rich('[[R]]')} /> is the distance from the center to the edge, so <span set:html={rich('[[D = 2R]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{D}{2}]]')} />. Doubling a radius gives the diameter, and halving a diameter gives the radius.</p>
  <p>For a right triangle, the diameter has a second identity: it equals the hypotenuse. The hypotenuse stretches across the circumcircle from one side to the other, through the center, so <span set:html={rich('[[D = c]]')} />. Combining the two relationships gives <span set:html={rich('[[c = 2R]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{c}{2}]]')} />, which is the formula this page is built on.</p>
  <p>A numerical example keeps the three lengths straight. In a triangle with <span set:html={rich('[[c = 18]]')} /> cm, the diameter is <span set:html={rich('[[D = 18]]')} /> cm and the circumradius is <span set:html={rich('[[R = 9]]')} /> cm. The hypotenuse, 18 cm, and the diameter, 18 cm, are the same line measured in two roles, while the radius, 9 cm, is half of that line.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 16: sec-vs-inradius -->
<section id="sec-vs-inradius" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Circumradius vs. Inradius of a Right Triangle</h2>
  <p>The circumradius and the inradius are the two radii of a right triangle, and they describe two different circles. The circumradius <span set:html={rich('[[R]]')} /> is the radius of the circumcircle, which passes through all three vertices. The inradius <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> is the radius of the incircle, which sits inside the triangle and touches all three sides. One circle surrounds the triangle, the other sits inside it.</p>
  <p>For a right triangle, the circumradius is <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{c}{2}]]')} />, while the inradius is <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{a + b - c}{2}]]')} />, where <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> are the legs and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the hypotenuse. The circumradius needs only the hypotenuse; the inradius needs all three sides, because how closely the incircle fits depends on the shape of the triangle, not just its longest side.</p>
  <p>The centers differ too. The circumcenter is the midpoint of the hypotenuse, on the boundary of the triangle. The incenter is inside the triangle, where the angle bisectors meet, at distance <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} /> from each side.</p>
  <p>Use the 6-8-10 triangle to see the difference in numbers. The circumradius is <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{10}{2} = 5]]')} /> cm, and the inradius is <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{6 + 8 - 10}{2} = \\frac{4}{2} = 2]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p>For problems about the incircle, use the <a href="/right-triangle-inradius-calculator/">right triangle inradius calculator</a>, which applies <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{a + b - c}{2}]]')} /> directly. The <a href="/right-triangle-radius-calculator/">right triangle radius calculator</a> covers both radii on one page when you need to compare them.</p>
  <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6">
    <table class="w-full text-sm my-6 border-collapse">
      <thead>
        <tr><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Feature</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Circumradius R</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Inradius r</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Circle</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Circumcircle</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Incircle</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Position</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Around the triangle</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Inside the triangle</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Contact</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Passes through vertices</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Tangent to sides</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Right triangle formula</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">c/2</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">(a+b-c)/2</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Center</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Circumcenter</td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Incenter</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
