</section>

<!-- Section 17: sec-pythagoras -->
<section id="sec-pythagoras" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Relationship Between Circumradius and the Pythagorean Theorem</h2>
  <p>The Pythagorean theorem states that in a right triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 + b^2 = c^2]]')} />, where <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> are the legs and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the hypotenuse. It is the link between the legs and the hypotenuse, and through the hypotenuse it is also a link to the circumradius.</p>
  <p>Since <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{c}{2}]]')} />, and the theorem gives <span set:html={rich('[[c = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}]]')} />, substitution gives <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{\\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}}{2}]]')} />. That combined formula finds the circumradius from the two legs in one step, without writing down the hypotenuse separately. It is the same two-step calculation as the legs section, written as a single expression.</p>
  <p>Check it with the 6-8-10 triangle. The legs give <span set:html={rich('[[\\sqrt{6^2 + 8^2} = \\sqrt{36 + 64} = \\sqrt{100} = 10]]')} /> cm for the hypotenuse, and halving gives <span set:html={rich('[[R = 5]]')} /> cm. The Pythagorean theorem supplies the hypotenuse, and the circumradius formula halves it.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 18: sec-verify -->
<section id="sec-verify" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Verify Your Circumradius Calculation</h2>
  <p>Four independent checks catch almost every error. Run at least one on every answer, and all four when the problem matters. The example below uses the 6-8-10 triangle, with <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[R = 5]]')} /> cm, throughout.</p>
  <h3>Check 1: Diameter Relationship</h3>
  <p>Double the circumradius and compare with the hypotenuse: <span set:html={rich('[[2R = c]]')} />. Here <span set:html={rich('[[2 \\times 5 = 10]]')} /> cm, which matches c exactly. If doubling your answer does not return the hypotenuse, the division by two went wrong.</p>
  <h3>Check 2: Midpoint Relationship</h3>
  <p>The circumcenter must sit halfway along the hypotenuse. Measure from either end of the hypotenuse to the center: each half must equal <span set:html={rich('[[R]]')} />. Here each half is 5 cm, matching the circumradius. A center that is not at the midpoint is not the circumcenter.</p>
  <h3>Check 3: Equal Distances</h3>
  <p>Verify <span set:html={rich('[[OA = OB = OC = R]]')} />. The center must be the same distance from all three vertices. In the 6-8-10 triangle, each distance is 5 cm. If the distance to the right-angle vertex differs from the distance to the hypotenuse endpoints, the center or the radius is wrong.</p>
  <h3>Check 4: Pythagorean Theorem</h3>
  <p>When the legs are known, verify <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 + b^2 = c^2]]')} /> and compare the resulting hypotenuse with <span set:html={rich('[[2R]]')} />. Here <span set:html={rich('[[6^2 + 8^2 = 36 + 64 = 100 = 10^2]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> cm, and <span set:html={rich('[[2R = 10]]')} /> cm agrees. This check tests the whole triangle, so it catches labeling errors the first check can miss.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 19: sec-properties -->
<section id="sec-properties" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Important Properties of Circumradius in Right Triangles</h2>
  <p>Nine properties follow directly from <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{c}{2}]]')} />. Each one doubles as a sense check on a new answer.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The circumradius is exactly half the hypotenuse, <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{c}{2}]]')} />, with no approximation involved.</li>
    <li>The hypotenuse is the diameter of the circumcircle, so <span set:html={rich('[[D = c]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 2R]]')} />.</li>
    <li>The circumcenter lies at the midpoint of the hypotenuse, on the triangle's longest side.</li>
    <li>The circumradius is always positive for a nondegenerate triangle, since the hypotenuse is a positive length.</li>
    <li>The circumradius equals half the hypotenuse, and since the hypotenuse is the longest side, the circumradius is less than the hypotenuse. Whether it is larger or smaller than a given leg depends on the triangle: in a 6-8-10 triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[R = 5]]')} /> cm is smaller than the leg of 8 cm, while in a 5-12-13 triangle, <span set:html={rich('[[R = 6.5]]')} /> cm is larger than the leg of 5 cm.</li>
    <li>Increasing the hypotenuse increases the circumradius proportionally, because <span set:html={rich('[[R]]')} /> is half of <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Doubling the hypotenuse doubles the circumradius: if <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> becomes <span set:html={rich('[[2c]]')} />, then <span set:html={rich('[[R]]')} /> becomes <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />.</li>
    <li>The legs are not needed when the hypotenuse is already known; the formula uses <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> alone.</li>
    <li>All three vertices are equally distant from the circumcenter, with <span set:html={rich('[[AO = BO = CO = R]]')} />.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<!-- Section 20: sec-mistakes -->
<section id="sec-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Calculating Circumradius</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Entering a leg instead of the hypotenuse: halving a leg gives half a leg, not the circumradius. In a 6-8-10 triangle, halving 6 cm gives 3 cm, while the true circumradius is 5 cm.</li>
    <li>Forgetting to divide by two: reporting the hypotenuse as the answer gives the diameter, not the radius. With <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> cm, the answer is 5 cm, not 10 cm.</li>
    <li>Treating the hypotenuse as the radius: this is the same error from the other direction. The hypotenuse spans the full width of the circle, so it is the diameter, and the radius is half of it.</li>
    <li>Confusing circumradius with inradius: the inradius of a 6-8-10 triangle is 2 cm, while the circumradius is 5 cm. The two circles are different, and their formulas are different too.</li>
    <li>Confusing the circumcenter with the right-angle vertex: the circumcenter is the midpoint of the hypotenuse, not vertex C. Vertex C is a point on the circle, at distance <span set:html={rich('[[R]]')} /> from the center.</li>
    <li>Assuming the center is always inside a triangle: in a right triangle the circumcenter lies on the hypotenuse, and in an obtuse triangle it lies outside. </li>
    <li>Using the formula on an arbitrary triangle: <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{c}{2}]]')} /> holds only for right triangles. For a general triangle, use <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{abc}{4A}]]')} />, where <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> is the area.</li>
    <li>Mixing measurement units: a hypotenuse in meters and an answer reported in centimeters differ by a factor of 100. Convert first, then halve.</li>
    <li>Using zero or negative hypotenuse values: a length cannot be zero or negative in a real triangle. A zero or negative input means the measurement is wrong.</li>
    <li>Confusing diameter and radius: the diameter is twice the radius. If your answer is twice the expected value, you probably reported <span set:html={rich('[[D]]')} /> instead of <span set:html={rich('[[R]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Assuming the circumcircle touches all three sides: the circumcircle passes through the vertices and touches the triangle only at those three points. The circle that touches the sides is the incircle.</li>
    <li>Forgetting that the circle passes through all three vertices: all three must lie on it, which is exactly what <span set:html={rich('[[OA = OB = OC]]')} /> guarantees.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

<!-- Section 21: sec-applications -->
<section id="sec-applications" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Practical Applications of Circumradius</h2>
  <p>The most direct use is geometry homework and classroom work, where circumradius problems test whether students can identify the hypotenuse and apply <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{c}{2}]]')} /> instead of reaching for a more complicated formula. Circle construction exercises use the same idea: given a right triangle, draw its circumcircle by halving the hypotenuse and setting a compass to that radius.</p>
  <p>Technical drawing uses the relationship when a right triangle must be placed inside a circular frame or when a circular arc must pass through three given points. Geometric modeling and coordinate geometry use the same fact to place a triangle's circumcenter at the midpoint of its hypotenuse.</p>
  <p>Teachers use the topic as a bridge lesson between triangles and circles, because it is one of the first places where the two subjects meet. Thales' theorem, the midpoint property, and the radius-diameter relationship all appear in a single picture, so the circumradius is a compact way to review them together.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 22: sec-practice -->
<section id="sec-practice" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Practice Problems With Answers</h2>
  <p>Try all six problems on paper before reading the answers. None of the values repeat the worked examples, and every solution is worked out in full below.</p>
  <h3>Problem 1: Basic Whole Number</h3>
  <p>A right triangle has hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c = 26]]')} /> cm. Find the circumradius <span set:html={rich('[[R]]')} />.</p>
  <h3>Problem 2: Decimal Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c = 9.4]]')} /> m, find the circumradius <span set:html={rich('[[R]]')} /> in meters.</p>
  <h3>Problem 3: Fractional Measurement</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c = \\frac{9}{4}]]')} /> ft, find <span set:html={rich('[[R]]')} /> as a fraction, then as a decimal.</p>
  <h3>Problem 4: Unit Conversion</h3>
  <p>A right triangle has hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c = 240]]')} /> cm. Find the circumradius in meters.</p>
  <h3>Problem 5: Find the Hypotenuse From Two Legs</h3>
  <p>A right triangle has legs <span set:html={rich('[[a = 9]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[b = 12]]')} /> cm. Find the hypotenuse, then the circumradius.</p>
  <h3>Problem 6: Circumradius or Inradius?</h3>
  <p>A right triangle has legs 5 cm and 12 cm and hypotenuse 13 cm. Find both the circumradius <span set:html={rich('[[R]]')} /> and the inradius <span set:html={rich('[[r]]')} />, and state which circle each one belongs to.</p>
  <h3>Practice Problem Answers</h3>
  <p>Answer 1: <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{26}{2} = 13]]')} /> cm. Check: <span set:html={rich('[[2 \\times 13 = 26]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p>Answer 2: <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{9.4}{2} = 4.7]]')} /> m. Check: <span set:html={rich('[[2 \\times 4.7 = 9.4]]')} /> m.</p>
  <p>Answer 3: <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{9/4}{2} = \\frac{9}{8}]]')} /> ft, which is 1.125 ft. Dividing by 2 doubles the denominator from 4 to 8.</p>
  <p>Answer 4: <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{240}{2} = 120]]')} /> cm, and 120 cm equals 1.2 m, since 100 cm is 1 m.</p>
  <p>Answer 5: <span set:html={rich('[[c = \\sqrt{9^2 + 12^2} = \\sqrt{81 + 144} = \\sqrt{225} = 15]]')} /> cm, then <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{15}{2} = 7.5]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p>Answer 6: The circumradius belongs to the circumcircle, <span set:html={rich('[[R = \\frac{13}{2} = 6.5]]')} /> cm, and the inradius belongs to the incircle, <span set:html={rich('[[r = \\frac{5 + 12 - 13}{2} = \\frac{4}{2} = 2]]')} /> cm. The circumcircle passes through the vertices; the incircle touches the sides.</p>
</section>

